speechtext:
I wish to endorse the view of the hon. member for Shelburne and Queens (Mr. Fielding) as regards approaching ministers of the Crown. I take this ground regarding lobbying in this House in respect to appointments to positions, that it is the privilege of any citizen in the Dominion of Canada to approach any minister of the Crown, member of Parliament or member of the Civil Service Com-

mission and to tell him about his -qualification for such position; but it is the privilege of the minister of the Crown-, member of Parliament, or commissioner to exercise his own judgment, show his toack-bone and show that there is some manhood in. him. Why should any person be -suppressed? Why should he -be chloroformed? Why should he be prevented from looking *at a minister except through s-moked glasses or at a Civil Service commissioner except through glasses equally opaque. Why should *a minister of the Crown, member of Parliament or commissioner not have the back-bone to say:	Here are the facts-;
I regard you as a proper person for the position, and I do not so regard the other party. Many of us can remember certain pre-election promises. If we get in, -such and such a person will get the position; and then if the wheel of fortune turns, we find that the same promise has been made to ten other people. We want backbone on the part of ministers and other high officials. Then, let any person approach them in any manner, -and the result will be all right. So far as the judges of the land are concerned, there are all sorts of stories in regard to people consulting judges in advance, but the judges, to their eternal credit, do n-ot yield- to such importuning, but rather count it against those who exercise undue influence.
